+## Ownership maintenance in the Murage vendor build
+
+`close-owned --session NAME --launch-id ID --json` closes only the evidenced launch. It first requests a launch-bound restore save and graceful close, then uses a bounded force fallback and confirms exhaustion. `close-only --session NAME` is a compatibility alias for the same transaction. `launch-group --owner-file PATH --status` observes ownership; `--stop --launch-id ID` uses the shared close transaction on either platform. Maintenance commands are not browser-automation MCP tools.
+
+`close --all`, `dashboard stop`, `/api/kill`, both restart branches and signal handlers use the same close transaction. Windows opens only the job name derived from the frozen launch ID (or validated legacy session and daemon PID). Unix closure needs the daemon's exhaustion proof, daemon retirement and empty final censuses. Missing ownership evidence never downgrades an ownership launch to legacy, and recorded process numbers never authorize orphan signals.
